window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][7] = "<span id=\"ex-c04-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C04-07 · From a number to a decision</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Estimate the annual revenue from a niche tool and decide whether a dedicated team is justified.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company requires $2 million of annual revenue to justify a dedicated team.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented.</em> 400,000 potential users; adoption between 2% and 8%, base 5%; price $120 a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the so-what bridge, including break-even adoption.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Threshold, compare, driver, narrow, act.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Base: 400,000 × 5% = 20,000 users × $120 = $2.4 million. Low: 400,000 × 2% = 8,000 × $120 = $0.96 million. High: 400,000 × 8% = 32,000 × $120 = $3.84 million. Break-even adoption: $2,000,000 ÷ $120 = 16,667 users, which is 4.2% of 400,000. The range straddles the $2 million threshold, and the base case is only slightly above it, so the estimate does not settle the decision. The driver is adoption, so the cheapest narrowing step is a small pre-order or fake-door test to measure interest. Action: run a four-week test and fund the team only if measured interest implies adoption above about 4.2%.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Fund a half-size team with an explicit review at six months.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The test delays a decision by four weeks and avoids committing a team to a base case that is barely above the threshold.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c04-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C04-08 · Units and capacity for video calls</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A company runs 2,000 concurrent video calls at its peak, each with four participants. Each participant uploads 1.5 Mbps and downloads 3 Mbps. Estimate the bandwidth the service must handle, and the data per hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Capacity planning for an internal tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As given (assumptions).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> State units carefully.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Capacity decomposition and unit conversion.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Participants: 2,000 × 4 = 8,000. Upload: 8,000 × 1.5 Mbps = 12,000 Mbps = 12 Gbps. Download: 8,000 × 3 Mbps = 24,000 Mbps = 24 Gbps. In bytes: 12 Gbps ÷ 8 = 1.5 GB/s inbound; 24 Gbps ÷ 8 = 3 GB/s outbound. Per hour: 1.5 GB/s × 3,600 = 5,400 GB = 5.4 TB inbound; 3 GB/s × 3,600 = 10,800 GB = 10.8 TB outbound. With 50% headroom, plan for about 18 Gbps in and 36 Gbps out.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use a media relay that forwards one stream per participant, which changes the download factor; ask engineering how the system is built.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Peak capacity costs more than average capacity, and under-provisioning shows up as dropped calls at the worst time.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c04-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C04-09 · Spot the flaw</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A candidate estimates daily pizza orders in a city of 1 million like this: &quot;1,000,000 people times 30% order pizza each day times one pizza is 300,000 orders a day.&quot; Critique and correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A practice transcript.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None given; use your own anchors.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Name each flaw.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Check units, per-capita plausibility and the stock-flow definition.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Flaws: (1) the 30% is &quot;per day,&quot; which implies 110 pizzas per person per year on average (0.3 × 365), implausible; (2) the candidate did not define whether an order is a pizza or a checkout; (3) there is no cross-check. A per-capita test exposes the error immediately. Correction: define an order as one checkout; assume 15% of people order pizza for delivery in a given week and place one order, so 150,000 orders a week, about 21,000 a day (150,000 ÷ 7), or 0.02 per person per day, roughly 8 a year, which is plausible. Cross-check from supply: 400 pizza places × 50 delivered orders a day = 20,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Estimate households instead of people, then orders per household.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A weekly share is easier to reason about than a daily one, and converting to a daily number is one extra step.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c04-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C04-10 · A five-minute drill with calibration</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In five minutes, estimate the daily trips on ride-hailing apps in a city of 5 million, and give an 80% confidence interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A timed spoken drill.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Include a cross-check and a range.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The estimation protocol, compressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Define a trip as one completed ride. Adults: 5,000,000 × 70% = 3,500,000. Share riding on a typical day: 4%: 140,000 riders. Trips per rider: 1.4: about 196,000, so about 200,000 trips. Cross-check from supply: 12,000 active drivers × 16 trips = 192,000, which agrees. Range: share of adults 3% to 6% gives 105,000 to 210,000 riders and about 150,000 to 300,000 trips. My 80% interval: 120,000 to 320,000 trips. Note which assumption dominates (the adult share) and what would calibrate it (trips in a similar city).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start from supply: drivers times trips, then check from demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> In five minutes you have time for one check; choose the one that is most independent of your main assumption.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: five-minute estimates.</em> Pick a prompt (the exercises and IQ-C04-01 to IQ-C04-11). Clarify, decompose, assume, compute, say the answer. Five minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: ten-minute estimates with a check.</em> Add a second route, a per-capita check, and a range.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: add the decision.</em> Add the so-what bridge, and include one &quot;critique a number&quot; drill (IQ-C04-07).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-8-timed-drills-and-calibration-practice\"></span>\n<h4>C04.8 Timed drills and calibration practice</h4>\n<p>Timed drills build speed; calibration builds honesty about your ranges. Calibration means that when you say a range holds the truth 80% of the time, it does. Research on judgment commonly finds that people's intervals are too narrow, so their ranges capture the truth less often than they claim: in one review, 90% intervals contained the true value only about half the time <code>[V: Soll and Klayman, Journal of Experimental Psychology: Learning, Memory, and Cognition 30(2), 299-314, 2004; McKenzie, Liersch and Yaniv, 2008]</code>. Train it with quantities you can check. For each, write an 80% interval before you compute, then compute:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Seconds in a week</td>\n<td>604,800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Minutes in a year</td>\n<td>525,600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Hours in ten years</td>\n<td>87,600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Days in a million seconds</td>\n<td>about 11.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Years in a billion seconds</td>\n<td>about 31.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>17 × 24</td>\n<td>408</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>12.5% of 640</td>\n<td>80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Weeks in 10,000 hours</td>\n<td>about 59.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Working years in 10,000 hours (2,000 hours a year)</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Kilobytes in a gigabyte (decimal)</td>\n<td>1,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Count how many of your ten intervals contain the answer. If fewer than eight do, your intervals are too narrow; widen them by about 50% and repeat with a fresh set. Track the hit rate in the journal over the three weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I define the quantity, unit and scope, and say stock or flow?</li>\n<li>Did I use three or four estimable factors?</li>\n<li>Did I state each assumption with a reason and a provenance?</li>\n<li>Did I use round numbers and carry units?</li>\n<li>Did I check with a second route and a per-capita test?</li>\n<li>Did I name the dominant assumption?</li>\n<li>Did I give a range and round to one or two figures?</li>\n<li>Did I say what decision the number serves?</li>\n<li>Did I recover cleanly from any arithmetic slip?</li>\n<li>Did I finish in the time?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to challenge your largest assumption at minute four (&quot;why 25%?&quot;) and to correct one of your numbers at minute six. Practice updating the whole chain aloud and saying how much the answer moves.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, your estimate and range, the dominant assumption, your score out of ten, the item to fix, and, for calibration, your hit rate. After six drills, look for the factor you most often get wrong, and write a better anchor for it.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next two weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Collect three numbers from your own work that people quote without a source: a market size, a traffic figure, a capacity claim. For each, ask for the decomposition, or rebuild it with the protocol (FW-C04-01) and a second route. Note where the figure and your rebuild disagree.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Size the opportunity for your team's next feature using the range method (FW-C04-06) and the so-what bridge (FW-C04-05). Write a one-page memo using the decision-memo template from C02 (FW-C02-06), with the threshold, the range, the driving assumption and the cheapest way to narrow it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Start a forecast log: before each launch or experiment, write your estimate of the key number with an 80% interval, then score it afterward. Do this five times.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Sanity-check one figure on a slide that someone else made, using a per-capita check and a bounds check, and tell the author what you found, kindly and with the arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the sizing memo (a time you used numbers to shape a decision), the forecast log (calibration and learning), and the slide check (catching an error and handling it well). Keep the numbers real.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c04-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Define the quantity first, then decompose it into three or four factors you can reason about, and say each assumption aloud with a provenance.</li>\n<li>Check every estimate by a second route and a per-capita test, and name the assumption that dominates the result.</li>\n<li>Round to one or two figures and give a range; false precision signals that you do not know what you do not know.</li>\n<li>Tie the number to a decision: name the threshold, compare the range with it, and say what would narrow the gap.</li>\n<li>Practice mental math and calibration, because arithmetic nerves and overconfident ranges cost more answers than reasoning does.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c04-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Lawrence Weinstein and John A. Adam, <em>Guesstimation</em> (2008). A collection of worked estimation problems in the Fermi tradition.</li>\n<li>Sanjoy Mahajan, <em>Street-Fighting Mathematics</em> (2010). Short, practical methods for getting approximate answers fast.</li>\n<li>Douglas W. Hubbard, <em>How to Measure Anything</em> (2nd edition, 2010). Calibrated estimates, ranges and the value of information.</li>\n<li>Philip E. Tetlock and Dan Gardner, <em>Superforecasting</em> (2015). The practice of forecasting with explicit probabilities and tracking accuracy.</li>\n<li>Daniel Kahneman, <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em> (2011). Includes the outside view and the planning fallacy, which bear on reference-class estimates.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-ii\"></span>\n";
